# =====================================================================
# .vercelignore  ·  WHAT VERCEL IS ALLOWED TO UPLOAD. Nothing else.
# Owner: deployment
#
# >>> WHY THIS FILE EXISTS — the thing that was actually live <<<
# Until this file was written there was no output directory setting and
# no ignore list, so the deployment was THE WHOLE REPOSITORY. Every one
# of these returned HTTP 200 on https://kuwait-sat1-hub.vercel.app:
#
#   /03-security/db/01_tables_rls.sql   the complete row-level-security
#                                       policy text, readable by anyone
#   /04-agents/GUARDRAILS.md            the threat model
#   /site-original/index.html           a COMPLETE copy of the app that
#                                       does not load js/ksat-shell.js,
#                                       so every gated "insider" section
#                                       is simply on screen for anyone
#   /02-back-end/prototype/index.html   a second ungated copy
#   /app-retag/login.html               a login form that accepts ANY
#                                       password, because js/config.js
#                                       404s from that path and
#                                       app-retag/js/data.js falls
#                                       through to its demo branch
#   /04-agents/tools/preflight.js       our own pre-demo checklist
#   /CONTRIBUTING.md, /CREDITS-AND-SOURCES.txt, /.gitleaksignore
#
# None of that is deleted. Nothing in this repository is deleted. It
# simply stops being uploaded. The team's decision is: SHIP ONLY THE APP.
#
# >>> THE SHAPE, AND WHY IT IS THIS SHAPE <<<
# .vercelignore is read with .gitignore semantics: patterns exclude, a
# leading ! re-includes, and THE LAST MATCHING LINE WINS. Two rules of
# that syntax decide everything below, and getting either one wrong
# deploys an empty site or deploys everything:
#
#   1. A pattern with no slash matches a NAME AT ANY DEPTH. So the bare
#      `*` on its own below excludes `css` and `css/ksat-theme.css` and
#      `04-agents/tools/preflight.js` alike. That is what we want as a
#      default: an allowlist has to start from nothing.
#
#   2. You cannot re-include a file whose PARENT DIRECTORY is still
#      excluded — the walker never descends into it, so `!css/**` alone
#      would ship nothing. Every directory therefore needs TWO lines,
#      `!css` to let the walker in and `!css/**` for its contents. If
#      you add a directory to the app later and only write one of the
#      two, the whole directory silently disappears from the deploy and
#      the site 404s its own stylesheet. Write both lines.
#
# The alternative shape, an anchored `/*`, only excludes root entries
# and would let anything nested through by default. Deliberately not
# used: a deny-by-default list should not have a depth at which it
# stops denying.
#
# >>> HOW TO CHECK THIS WITHOUT GUESSING <<<
# `npx vercel build` then look in .vercel/output/static — that is the
# real answer. Failing that, after the next deployment curl one path
# from each list above and expect 200 for the app and not-200 for the
# rest. There is also a second belt in vercel.json: redirects that send
# the old directory paths back to `/` before the filesystem is ever
# consulted, for the case where a deployment route we did not predict
# ignores this file. Remove neither on the strength of the other.
#
# vercel.json itself cannot carry comments — it is strict JSON and
# Vercel's schema sets additionalProperties: false, so even a "//" key
# fails the deployment. The reasoning for what is in it (the two new
# headers, why Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy is deliberately absent, the
# redirects, the cron) is written up in api/README.md section 4.
# =====================================================================


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1 · Deny everything, at every depth. Read rule 1 above before editing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
*


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2 · Re-include the app, and only the app. Directory line first, then
#     its contents — read rule 2 above before adding to this list.
#
#     vercel.json is here because a CLI deployment that does not upload
#     it loses every header, redirect and cron in it. It is not served:
#     Vercel strips it from the output (verified — /vercel.json is a 404
#     on the live site today, while every other root file is a 200).
#
#     api/ MUST stay in this list. /api/monitor is the nightly sweep
#     cron; excluding it turns the cron into a 404 every night at 02:00
#     and nobody finds out until a run sits 'running' in front of a
#     judge.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
!index.html
!site.webmanifest
!vercel.json

#     researcher.html is the workspace a researcher lands on after signing
#     in. It is listed here on purpose and it is safe to serve: the file is
#     an EMPTY workspace. No frame, no coordinate and no mission is in it.
#     Everything it shows arrives over an authenticated PostgREST call that
#     row level security answers — see 03-security/db/13_payload_archive.sql.
#     Fetch it signed out and you get a page that tells you nothing.
!researcher.html

!api
!api/**

!assets
!assets/**

!css
!css/**

!js
!js/**

!vendor
!vendor/**


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3 · Re-exclude the documentation that lives INSIDE the app folders.
#     These are notes to the team, not part of the site, and /js/README.md
#     is a 200 on the live site right now — it tells a reader which key
#     is in js/config.js and what the security assumption behind it is.
#     True and fine to say in the repo; no reason to serve it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
js/README.md
assets/README.md
api/README.md


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4 · Named last, on purpose. Every one of these is already excluded by
#     the `*` above; they are written out anyway so that this list is a
#     readable statement of intent, and so that they survive a future
#     `!something` line added carelessly higher up — last match wins.
#
#     If you ever genuinely need to ship one of these, DELETE ITS LINE
#     HERE rather than adding another `!` above it. Two lines arguing
#     about the same path is how a directory comes back by accident.
#
#     Several of these do not exist in the repository today (tests/,
#     docs/, db/ at the root, and .tmp/ which is gitignored). They are
#     listed because the names are already used elsewhere in the tree
#     and a root-level one would otherwise ship the day someone makes it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
01-front-end/
02-back-end/
03-security/
04-agents/
app-retag/
site-original/
tools/
tests/
docs/
db/
.tmp/

# Root-level loose files and every markdown file anywhere. The app does
# not load a single .md, so this cannot take anything the site needs.
*.md
CREDITS-AND-SOURCES.txt
kuwaitsat1-team-photo.webp
.env
.env.*
.gitleaksignore
.gitattributes
.nojekyll
